// campaigns domain -- the Go-backed sending surface (memql#3348).
//
// WHY THESE ARE BUILTINS AND NOT MUTATIONS. Everything else an operator
// does to a campaign is a plain mutation, and that is the right default:
// the concept treatment (browsable, view-composable, authorization-gated)
// comes free, and a mutation is auditable in a way a Go call is not. Seven
// operations cannot be expressed that way, and each fails for a specific
// reason rather than for convenience:
//
//   startSend    A PREFLIGHT SPANNING SEVERAL ROWS, then TWO WRITES.
//                Is a sender registered on this node? Is one-click
//                unsubscribe configured? Is the template marked ready? Is
//                the audience non-empty and inside the ceiling? Every one
//                is a read, and a mutation body cannot read. It then has
//                to write the campaign (owned, the operator's row) AND
//                the send job (clusterOwner, the engine's) -- two writes,
//                one per mutation being the language's contract. The
//                usual answer, an automation on the first write, does not
//                work here: the automation actor is neither the owner nor
//                a cluster owner, so it could not write the job, and
//                making the job writable by it means making it writable
//                by anyone -- and whichever row says "send campaign X as
//                user Y" is an instruction the engine carries out under
//                Y's authority.
//
//   scheduleSend Same preflight, same two writes -- and one more reason
//                (memql#3459). "Which campaigns are due?" cannot be asked of
//                v1:campaigns:campaign at all: the owned tier injects
//                ownerUserId==actor.userId into every read and has no
//                cluster-owner bypass, so no clock-driven scan over an
//                operator's rows is expressible. The schedule therefore has
//                to be recorded on the engine's own clusterOwner-tier job
//                row, which only Go holding a campaign it already read under
//                the caller's actor may write.
//
//   pauseSend    Same two-writes problem, campaign plus job.
//   resumeSend
//
//   suppress     Writes the CLUSTER-WIDE do-not-mail list, whose key is a
//   recordFeedback SHA-256 digest of the address. The digesting has to
//                happen server-side or the address travels instead of the
//                digest, which defeats the point of a digest-keyed list.
//                Both also carry an admin gate that is a role check
//                rather than a row predicate, because the rows they write
//                have no owner to gate on.
//
//   ingestFeedback  PARSING, of a shape a filter expression has no business
//                attempting: a DSN is folded RFC 822 field groups inside a
//                MIME report, and an SES payload is JSON nested inside JSON
//                inside a string (memql#3461). Its gate is the one exception
//                to the paragraph above -- it is CONFIGURATION rather than a
//                role, because the call asserts nothing. Every address and
//                every verdict comes out of a body a third party signed with
//                a secret the operator configured, arriving at a source the
//                operator allowlisted, in a format the operator named. The
//                most an arbitrary caller achieves is re-processing a
//                webhook that was already trusted, which is idempotent;
//                requiring admin would have meant every deployment minting a
//                service-account credential for an automation to present,
//                for no property this does not already have.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS THE FIRST READ, in every one of the campaign-scoped
// three. `campaignById` is owned-tier, so it returns a row only to its
// owner; a caller who cannot read the campaign gets "not found" and
// nothing further happens. Every value that reaches the send job -- the
// owner, the audience, the template -- is copied off THAT row rather than
// taken from an argument, so a caller can only ever start a send as a
// user they could already act as.
//
// WHICH OF THESE ARE @sdk, AND WHY (memql#4239). @sdk is a statement about the
// PUBLIC CLIENT SURFACE -- `make sdk-gen` emits a typed Go + TypeScript method
// only for a builtin that carries it -- and nothing else: it has no engine
// effect, and every builtin here is reachable on the wire by name with or
// without it. The four campaign-scoped actions carry it. They are the
// operator's own verbs on their own campaign, the portal's buttons already
// drive them, and their authorization is the owned-tier read above, so a
// typed method advertises nothing a caller could not already do to a row
// they own. The other three do not carry it, for two different reasons:
// suppress and recordFeedback are admin-gated writes to the cluster-wide
// list that no client drives yet (marking them is a one-line decision for
// whoever adds the first caller, not a statement that they are internal);
// ingestFeedback is the automation's door, and its argument is a staged
// inbound row id no client holds.

@description("Preflight and start a campaign send. Refuses -- rather than partially sending -- when no email sender is registered on the node, when one-click unsubscribe is not configured (MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET / _BASE_URL), when the template is not marked ready, or when the audience is empty or at the MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_MAX_AUDIENCE ceiling. Authorization is the owned-tier read of the campaign: a caller who cannot read it cannot start it. Returns the recipient count the send will work through.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.campaigns.startSend")
builtin campaignStartSend {
  campaignId  string!  @description("The campaign to send. The caller must own it.")
}

@description("Commit a campaign to a time and enqueue the send job that will fire at it (memql#3459). Runs the SAME preflight as campaignStartSend -- sender registered, one-click unsubscribe configured, template marked ready, audience non-empty and inside the ceiling -- because the whole value of scheduling is finding out now rather than at 3am. The job it writes is inert: it sits in the 'scheduled' status until the drain worker sees that the campaign's scheduledAt has passed, and the campaign row is the authority on that time, so moving the date with updateCampaign moves the send. A time in the past is refused; use campaignStartSend to send now. Authorization is the owned-tier read of the campaign, exactly as for starting one by hand.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.campaigns.scheduleSend")
builtin campaignScheduleSend {
  campaignId   string!  @description("The campaign to schedule. The caller must own it.")
  scheduledAt  string!  @description("When the send should begin, RFC 3339 (e.g. 2026-08-14T09:00:00Z). Interpreted as an instant, not a local wall clock -- a value with no offset is read as UTC.")
}

@description("Pause a running campaign send. The delivery ledger is untouched, so resuming continues exactly where it stopped -- 'where it stopped' is the set of recipients with no delivery row, not a cursor that could go stale.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.campaigns.pauseSend")
builtin campaignPauseSend {
  campaignId  string!  @description("The campaign to pause. The caller must own it.")
}

@description("Resume a paused campaign send. Does not re-stamp startedAt: the gap between startedAt and completedAt is how long the run took, and resetting it on resume would erase the pause it exists to reveal.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.campaigns.resumeSend")
builtin campaignResumeSend {
  campaignId  string!  @description("The campaign to resume. The caller must own it.")
}

@description("Add an address to the CLUSTER-WIDE do-not-mail list, which outranks every audience and is consulted at the point of send. Admin or cluster owner only: the list spans every operator in the deployment, so adding to it is deployment policy rather than an owner-scoped action. The address is digested server-side and never stored -- the row keeps the digest and the domain, so 'which domains are bouncing' stays answerable while 'who unsubscribed' does not.")
@executor("integration.campaigns.suppress")
builtin campaignSuppress {
  email   string!  @description("The address to suppress. Normalized (trimmed, lowercased) and SHA-256 digested before it reaches the graph.")
  reason  string  @description("unsubscribed | hard_bounce | complaint | manual. Defaults to manual.")
  note    string  @description("Free-text provenance. Never put the address here -- it is stored verbatim.")
}

@description("Record a bounce or complaint the mail provider reported. Admin or cluster owner only. A hard_bounce or complaint suppresses the address cluster-wide and the next send converges the operator's own recipient row onto that verdict; audience membership is NOT deleted, because deleting it destroys the audit trail and lets the next import resurrect a dead address. A soft_bounce is transient and does NOT suppress -- the per-recipient retry budget is what bounds those. This is the MANUAL door -- an operator entering a report by hand, or a bespoke automation presenting an admin service-account credential. The automated path is campaignIngestFeedback, which parses a real provider payload instead of being handed a verdict.")
@executor("integration.campaigns.recordFeedback")
builtin campaignRecordFeedback {
  email       string!  @description("The address the provider reported.")
  kind        string!  @description("hard_bounce | soft_bounce | complaint.")
  campaignId  string  @description("The campaign the report came from, when the provider named one. Recorded on the suppression row so a deliverability review can attribute complaints to a send.")
  note        string  @description("The provider's own classification string. Never the address.")
}

@description("Read a mail provider's bounce or complaint webhook off a staged v1:platform:inboundRequest row and apply it to the cluster-wide suppression list (memql#3461). Understands RFC 3464 delivery status notifications -- the standard format every SMTP-era relay including Microsoft Graph produces -- and Amazon SES feedback over SNS, which additionally carries the complaint feedback loop a DSN structurally cannot. Hard vs soft comes from the provider's own classification (the DSN's Status class or Action, SES's bounceType), never from the diagnostic text. GATED BY CONFIGURATION rather than by role: the row's source must be listed in MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_FEEDBACK_SOURCES and the delivery must carry signatureVerified=true, so the most an arbitrary caller can do is re-process a webhook the operator already trusted. A payload that cannot be read stamps the inbound row 'failed' with the reason AND errors, so it is queryable rather than dropped; a payload that reads fine and carries no bounce (a delivery receipt, an SNS subscription confirmation) stamps 'processed' and says which it was.")
@executor("integration.campaigns.ingestFeedback")
builtin campaignIngestFeedback {
  inboundRequestId  string!  @description("The staged v1:platform:inboundRequest row to read. In the shipped automation this is event.payload.id.")
}

@description("Import recipients into an audience from a CSV file already uploaded to the Library (memql#4822). The file is read SERVER-SIDE under the caller's own actor, so a file the caller cannot read is a file this cannot import -- the artifact id is not a capability. The header row must carry an `email` column (case-insensitive; `displayName` and `name` are also recognized) and EVERY OTHER COLUMN lands verbatim in the recipient's `fields` map, reachable from a template as {{fields.<key>}}. Per row: the address is normalized and shape-validated, deduplicated against the audience's existing recipients AND against earlier rows of the same file (first occurrence wins). The import refuses WHOLE when the resulting roster would exceed MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_MAX_AUDIENCE -- it never silently truncates, because a partially-imported list is one nobody knows is partial. Returns {added, duplicates, invalid, total} plus up to 20 sample invalid lines with their line numbers, so the operator's next action is fixing the file rather than guessing at it. Each added recipient also gets a consent grant event with source 'import'.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.campaigns.importRecipients")
builtin campaignImportRecipients {
  audienceId  string!  @description("The audience to import into. The caller must be able to read it.")
  artifactId  string!  @description("v1:library:artifact.id of the uploaded CSV. Read under the caller's actor -- an artifact the caller cannot read is not found.")
  hasHeader   bool  @description("Whether the first row is a header. Defaults true. A file with no header cannot be imported: the column mapping IS the header, and guessing which column holds addresses is how an import mails the wrong list.")
}

@description("Send one test copy of a campaign to a named address (memql#4822). Renders the campaign's template against a synthetic recipient -- display name 'Test Recipient', the address you name, and the `fields` of the audience's first real recipient when one exists, so {{fields.*}} show the shape they will actually have. The subject is prefixed '[Test] ', the message goes through the campaign's resolved sending identity, and the unsubscribe footer carries an obviously-inert token. It writes NO delivery row and touches NO counter, so a test send can never make a campaign look partly sent; it does consume the ordinary send-rate token bucket, because a test is a real message to a real mailbox. Returns the list of merge tags it could not resolve -- the check that catches a typo'd {{fields.compnay}} before the whole audience gets it. `to` is REQUIRED and never defaults to the caller's own address: a builtin that mails somewhere you did not name is one you have to remember the default of.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.campaigns.testSend")
builtin campaignTestSend {
  campaignId  string!  @description("The campaign to render. The caller must be able to read it.")
  to          string!  @description("Where to send the test. Required and validated -- there is deliberately no default.")
}

@description("The outcome breakdown for one campaign (memql#4823): recipients, pending, sent, failed, the skipped bucket split into suppressed / unsubscribed / other, hard bounces, complaints, one-click unsubscribes, and opens and clicks as both total and unique. Computed SERVER-SIDE from the delivery ledger, the campaign's consent events and its engagement events -- every bucket that can be an exact COUNT is one, at any audience size. This replaces counting a capped page of rows in the browser, which under-reported every campaign past the page bound and did so silently. Two figures are honest about their limits rather than rounded into a number: a unique open/click count is folded from a bounded read and is reported as UNMEASURED rather than as a wrong number when that bound is reached, and soft bounces are absent entirely because nothing measures them per campaign -- an absent figure and a zero are different answers.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.campaigns.stats")
builtin campaignStats {
  campaignId  string!  @description("The campaign to report on. The caller must be able to read it.")
}

@description("Send ONE message to ONE recipient through the campaign machinery -- the marketing lane's primitive for event-triggered email (memql#4829, program P5). Suppression is checked at the point of send, the RFC 8058 unsubscribe pair is attached, the resolved sending identity is applied, and the outcome is ledgered exactly as a campaign send is. It exists because the two event-email lanes are the only sanctioned shapes for automated mail: operational recipients ride the transactional outbox (allowlist enforced, no unsubscribe, no suppression) and marketing recipients ride this. There is deliberately NO free-form sendEmail builtin, and this is not one: it takes a template and an audience recipient, not a subject and a body.")
@executor("integration.campaigns.sendToRecipient")
builtin campaignSendToRecipient {
  templateId        string!  @description("v1:campaigns:template.id supplying the subject and bodies.")
  recipientId       string!  @description("v1:campaigns:recipient.id to send to. Its subscription state and the cluster suppression list are both consulted before anything leaves.")
  senderIdentityId  string   @description("Optional v1:campaigns:senderIdentity.id to send as. Empty resolves to the env-configured default, exactly as a campaign's does.")
  emailRuleId       string   @description("Optional v1:campaigns:emailRule.id this send came from, stamped on the ledger row so 'which rule mailed this person' is answerable.")
}

@description("Generate an event-email rule's automation construct and arm it (memql#4829). The generator is a DETERMINISTIC template over the rule's form -- the LLM authoring path stays off, because a rule that mails strangers is not a place for a model to improvise and a deterministic generator is one a person can read the output of. The generated .memql goes through the ordinary authoring pipeline: bundle, validate, activate. Activation takes effect IMMEDIATELY rather than at next boot. The rule row records the bundle and construct it produced; on refusal it records the engine's own sentence on lastError and the rule goes to 'failed' rather than silently staying draft.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.emailrules.activate")
builtin campaignActivateEmailRule {
  emailRuleId  string!  @description("The rule to generate and arm. The caller must own it: the generated construct runs under the AUTHOR's envelope, so who armed it decides what it can read.")
}

@description("Retire an event-email rule's generated construct (memql#4829). The automation stops firing and the bundle is retired; the RULE row survives with its history, because a rule somebody turned off is a different thing from one that never existed. Used by delete and by edit-and-regenerate, which retires the old bundle before arming the new one -- so a rule is never armed twice.")
@sdk
@executor("integration.emailrules.retire")
builtin campaignRetireEmailRule {
  emailRuleId  string!  @description("The rule whose construct should be retired.")
}

@description("Fire one event-email rule for one triggering row. THE GENERATED AUTOMATION'S ONLY STEP, and it is deliberately the only thing that automation does (memql#4829). The alternative -- generating lane-specific DSL with recipient loops and conditionals per rule -- would put the interesting logic in text a template assembles, where every bug is a bug in a string. Here the generated construct carries exactly what only it can carry (the @trigger binding a concept at load time, which is the whole reason a construct is generated at all) and the decisions live in Go, where they are read, tested and fixed once for every rule. Not @sdk and not client-callable: its caller is an authored automation running under the rule author's own envelope, and it resolves recipients, evaluates the lane and sends. Reached only through the construct the generator wrote.")
@executor("integration.emailrules.fire")
builtin emailRuleFire {
  emailRuleId  string!  @description("v1:campaigns:emailRule.id whose form decides what happens. Baked into the generated construct as a literal, so a rule can only ever fire itself.")
  nodeId       string   @description("The triggering row's id, bound from the event payload by the automation's args block.")
  event        object   @description("The whole triggering event envelope, forwarded by the generated step. Carries the row payload, which is where a row_address rule reads its recipient from -- reading the field off the EVENT rather than re-reading the row is what keeps the rule working on a concept whose rows the author's own envelope could not read back.")
}
